(eval `(do ,@(source "site.sx")))

(chapter "language.sx"
  (section "Reading code")
  (p "Netscheme is a Lisp, so its code is made of lists written in parentheses."
     " A list whose first item names a function calls it with the rest as"
     " arguments: " (code "(+ 1 2)") " is 3, and " (code "(* 2 (+ 1 2))") " is 6."
     " A semicolon starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.")

  (section "Values")
  (entry "42, -1.5" "Numbers. There's one kind, a 64-bit float, and whole numbers print without a decimal point.")
  (entry "\"text\"" "Strings. Inside one, " (code "\\\"") " is a quote, " (code "\\\\")
         " a backslash, " (code "\\n") " a new line and " (code "\\t") " a tab.")
  (entry "true, false, nil" (code "nil") " means nothing, and is also the empty list."
         " Only " (code "false") " and " (code "nil") " count as false: 0 and"
         " " (code "\"\"") " are true.")
  (entry "name" "Symbols name variables. Evaluating one looks the variable up.")
  (entry ":name" "Keywords evaluate to themselves. Elements use them for attributes,"
         " and dictionaries often use them as keys.")
  (entry "(1 2 3)" "Lists. To write one as data rather than a call, quote it: " (code "'(1 2 3)") ".")
  (entry "(dict :a 1 :b 2)" "Dictionaries, which map keys to values. Keys can be numbers,"
         " strings or keywords.")
  (entry "(fn (x) (* x x))" "Functions, which are values like any other.")
  (entry "(p \"hi\")" "Elements: what the element functions return, and what a page is made of.")

  (section "Special forms")
  (p "A few forms aren't function calls, because they don't evaluate all their"
     " arguments first, or because they define names:")
  (entry "(define name value)" "Defines a variable.")
  (entry "(define (name params...) body...)" "Defines a function. It's shorthand for"
         " " (code "(define name (fn (params...) body...))") ".")
  (entry "(fn (params...) body...)" "Makes a function. Its body's last value is"
         " what it returns. A parameter after " (code "&") " collects the remaining"
         " arguments as a list.")
  (entry "(define-state name initial)" "Defines a variable that keeps its value"
         " when the page runs again. See " (a :href "state.sx" "State and events") ".")
  (entry "(set! name value)" "Changes an existing variable, or state.")
  (entry "(if test then else)" "Evaluates " (code "then") " if the test is true, and"
         " otherwise " (code "else") ", or gives " (code "nil") " if there's no else.")
  (entry "(cond (test body...) ... (else body...))" "Tries each test in turn, and"
         " evaluates the body of the first that's true.")
  (entry "(let ((name value) ...) body...)" "Names some values for the body. Each"
         " value can use the names before it.")
  (entry "(and a b ...), (or a b ...)" "Stop at the first false value, or the first"
         " true one, and give that value.")
  (entry "(do a b ...)" "Evaluates each in turn and gives the last.")
  (entry "(quote x), 'x" "Gives " (code "x") " itself, without evaluating it.")
  (entry "`(a ,b ,@c)" "Quasiquote: like quote, but " (code ",") " evaluates one"
         " part, and " (code ",@") " evaluates a list and splices it in. See"
         " " (a :href "code-as-data.sx" "Code is data") ".")
  (entry "(eval x)" "Evaluates " (code "x") ", then evaluates the result as code.")

  (section "Functions")
  (p "Functions close over the variables around them, so a function can make"
     " another function that remembers something:")
  (example
    '(let ((adder (fn (n) (fn (x) (+ x n))))
           (add-ten (adder 10)))
       (p "Adding ten to each: " (interpose ", " (map add-ten '(1 2 3))))))
  (p "Rest parameters make variadic functions, and " (code "apply") " calls a"
     " function with a list of arguments:")
  (example
    '(let ((average (fn (& xs) (/ (apply + xs) (length xs)))))
       (p "The average of 3, 4 and 8 is " (average 3 4 8) ".")))

  (section "When things go wrong")
  (p "An error while a page loads, like calling something that isn't a function"
     " or misspelling a name, replaces the page with a message saying what went"
     " wrong. An error in an event handler shows in a banner below the toolbar,"
     " and the page stays as it was.")
  (p "Pages also have limits on how much work they can do, so a mistake can't"
     " freeze the browser:")
  (div :padding-left 18 :margin 12
    (p :margin 4 "• " (b "A million evaluation steps") " for each run of a page, and each event handler.")
    (p :margin 4 "• " (b "Ten thousand levels of nesting") ", so runaway recursion stops quickly.")
    (p :margin 4 "• " (b "A million characters or items") " in any string or list a builtin makes."))
  (p "See " (a :href "runaway.sx" "Runaway code") " for what happens when a page goes over."))
